WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:09.790
In our previous video, we discussed about
what a lens is and also its various

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.230
types.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:16.869
It is bounded by two surfaces… one of them
is spherical and the other?

00:00:16.869 --> 00:00:19.080
Yes it is spherical or plane!

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.939
These are the different TYPES of lenses!

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:26.710
The two specific lenses that we are going
to cover in this course are called

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:30.329
biconvex and biconcave lenses.

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:34.720
Each one is bounded by two transparent
SPHERICAL surfaces.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.609
Hence the word BI is used… it means two!

00:00:37.609 --> 00:00:41.590
In a biconvex
lens, the two spherical surfaces are curved

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:47.090
“inwards”, and in a biconcave lens,
they are curved “outwards”.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:52.289
One more way to distinguish between a convex
and a concave lens is that a convex lens is

00:00:52.289 --> 00:00:57.141
THICKER at the centre and THINNER
at the edges, while the concave lens is thicker

00:00:57.141 --> 00:01:00.800
at the edges and thinner at the
centre.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.730
Why do we use lenses?

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.610
What does a lens do?

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:09.000
In our previous video we saw a
few examples where lenses are used…

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.490
In binoculars and magnifying glasses for
instance...

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:14.280
But how does a lens function?

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.020
Well the main purpose of the lens is
to REFRACT the light in SOME PATTERN.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.070
There are basically two patterns:
CONVERGENCE and DIVERGENCE.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:28.159
But before studying these two patterns, we
first need to understand how the

00:01:28.159 --> 00:01:30.180
light is refracted by a lens.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.140
Consider a convex lens, and suppose a ray
of light is

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.119
incident onto it at an angle.

00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:39.149
This is the normal at the point where the
light ray

00:01:39.149 --> 00:01:40.970
is incident.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:45.869
Now consider a point object here from where
this ray is emerging.

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:48.909
What will happen when it enters the lens?

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:53.270
This ray is travelling from one medium to
another at an angle, so after entering

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.700
the lens, its speed will change which will
result in its BENDING.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.460
Will the speed
inside the lens increase or decrease?

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.969
Where will the light bend?

00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:07.200
Well that will depend on the incident medium!

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.789
Suppose this medium is air.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:12.450
So
from this table we can see that the refractive

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.810
index of glass is much greater
than air.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:19.640
This means that glass is optically denser
than air.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.780
Hence the speed of the ray of light will DECREASE
in glass and because of that,

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.340
it will bend TOWARDS the normal!

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:27.690
Alright!

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.760
So now what will happen when this refracted
ray has reached the

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.640
other spherical surface of the lens?

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:37.849
From there the ray will be refracted again
as it enters another medium which is

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:39.580
air again.

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:43.470
You see that this time, it bent AWAY from
the normal.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.390
Can you tell
me why?

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:50.710
Air is optically denser than glass and hence
after entering air, speed of the ray

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:53.980
increases, and it bends AWAY from the normal.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:58.400
And this is how the light ray is
refracted by a convex lens!

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.390
Here only one ray is emerging from the point
object.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:05.409
But in reality there are
many rays that emerge from this point object

00:03:05.409 --> 00:03:08.000
and not only one!

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.090
All these after striking the lens will be
refracted in the same way.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.050
Did you see
something strange?

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.989
Yes, all the rays meet at a common point!

00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:23.300
Or we can say that all the rays are
FOCUSSED or CONVERGED at a single point.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:27.019
And this is one of the patterns that we were
talking about!

00:03:27.019 --> 00:03:31.470
Convex lens bends the light rays from a point
object in such a way that after

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.780
refraction, all these rays converge at a single
point.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.080
And this is the point where
the image of the point object is formed!

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:44.470
If we were to place a screen here at
this location, then the image will be formed

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:45.830
on that screen.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:50.970
Since the convex lens CONVERGES all the light
rays at a single point, we also

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.640
call it a CONVERGING LENS.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.840
And what about a concave lens?

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.090
How do they refract light rays?

00:03:58.090 --> 00:03:59.690
We will see
that in the next lesson!

